.TH RUFUSARM64-DEVICE-BACKUP 1 "26 July 2026" "RufusArm64 0.14.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rufusarm64-device-backup \- save a verified raw, VHD, or VHDX image of a drive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rufusarm64-device-backup
.B --device
.I /dev/DEVICE
.B --output
.I /absolute/path/drive.img
.RI [ --format " raw|vhd|vhdx" ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B rufusarm64-device-backup
.B --device
.I /dev/DEVICE
.B --output
.I /absolute/path/drive.vhdx
.B --format vhdx
.B --dry-run
.RI [ --json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rufusarm64-device-backup
captures the exact reported capacity of one explicitly selected whole block
device into a newly created image. The source is opened read-only and
exclusively, bound to its kernel identity and capacity, and revalidated before
and after every externally executed container phase.
.PP
The default
.B raw
format is a byte-for-byte image. The
.B vhd
and
.B vhdx
formats are dynamic sparse containers created by a trusted, root-owned
.B qemu-img
utility. The source and private output are passed to that utility only through
inherited file descriptors. Backing files, compression, encryption, salvage,
shared-write, fixed containers, and caller-supplied converter options are not
accepted.
.PP
Container capture hashes the held raw source, converts into an owner-only
same-directory temporary file, compares guest-visible bytes against the held
source, performs the supported non-repairing consistency check, hashes and
synchronizes the container, and revalidates the source before publication.
VHDX requires a successful consistency check. QEMU does not provide that check
for VHD, so the report records it as unsupported rather than claiming it ran.
.PP
The completed image is published atomically without replacement only after all
required checks succeed. Existing destinations are never replaced. Incomplete
output is removed after failure or cancellation.
.PP
The destination must reside on a different physical disk from the selected
source. Raw capture requires the source capacity. VHD and VHDX dry-run planning
uses QEMU's fully allocated bound for conservative free-space admission; the
smaller sparse-container estimate is informational only. Mounted removable
filesystems are unmounted before capture unless
.B --no-unmount
is supplied.
.SH GRAPHICAL WORKFLOW
Select a removable drive and choose
.BR "Save drive image…" .
Choose Raw image, Dynamic VHD, or Dynamic VHDX before selecting a new
destination. RufusArm64 runs an unprivileged plan and displays the exact source,
format, capacity, destination directory, conservative required bytes, available
bytes, and any informational container minimum.
.PP
Before authentication it requires the exact phrase
.B SAVE /dev/DEVICE TO /absolute/path/drive.ext .
The dialog reports separate capture, source-hash, conversion, and output-hash
phases. A successful report includes source and output SHA-256 digests, output
size, content-comparison status, consistency status, and the published path.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --device " /dev/DEVICE"
Select the whole source disk. Partitions and paths outside
.I /dev
are refused.
.TP
.BI --output " PATH"
Absolute path for the new image file. The path must not already exist.
.TP
.BI --format " raw|vhd|vhdx"
Select the output format. The default is
.BR raw .
VHD and VHDX are always dynamic sparse containers with fixed converter policy.
.TP
.BI --expected-identity " TOKEN"
Require the exact identity token returned by
.BR "rufusarm64-cli list --json" .
This is mandatory with
.B --yes
or
.BR --allow-fixed .
.TP
.B --dry-run
Validate source metadata, format support, converter availability, destination
storage, conservative free-space bounds, and collision state without opening
the source device.
.TP
.B --json
Emit one deterministic schema-2 JSON plan or report.
.TP
.B --progress-json
Emit bounded schema-2 phase-labelled progress records on standard error while
retaining the final deterministic report on standard output.
.TP
.B --yes
Skip terminal confirmation. Accepted only with an exact expected identity.
.TP
.B --allow-fixed
Permit a non-removable whole disk for a direct terminal invocation. Root-disk
and protected-mount refusals remain in force.
.TP
.B --no-unmount
Refuse mounted filesystems instead of unmounting eligible removable mounts.
.TP
.BR --version , " -v"
Print the installed version.
.SH SAFETY
The source is never opened for writing, but coherent capture normally requires
its filesystems to be unmounted. QEMU receives descriptor paths rather than the
selected source or final destination pathname. No final pathname appears before
verified completion. Manually created image files remain user data after package
removal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rufusarm64 (1),
.BR rufusarm64-cli (1),
.BR rufusarm64-device-qualify (1)
